## CRITICAL RULES

### 1. PLANS

Location `./doc/plans/PLAN-<description>.md` — the only valid location (hard stop 1). Before any Write of a plan file, check that the path starts with `doc/plans/`.

**Loom plans:** do NOT create one unless the user explicitly requests loom orchestration ("use loom", "loom run", "create a loom plan") or invokes `/loom-plan-writer`. When you do, ALWAYS author it via `/loom-plan-writer` — that skill is canonical for all plan mechanics: pre-planning (explore, knowledge review, sandbox questions, `loom repair`), stage YAML (`stage_type`, `working_dir`, `model`, `reasoning_effort`), acceptance/artifacts/wiring design, path resolution, parallelization/hierarchy/ultracode choice, and validation with `loom plan verify`. Do not write loom YAML from memory.

**Executing a stage:** your signal file carries the stage's tasks and completion checklist (memory recording, wiring verification, mini adversarial code review, commit steps). Treat the signal as binding and work it to completion.

### 2. NO PLACEHOLDERS

No incomplete markers, stubs, `pass` bodies, empty functions, or pseudocode. Write complete code now. Unknown? Ask (interactive) or resolve per Engineering Discipline A (autonomous). Complex? Decompose.

### 3. CONTEXT AT 75% = STOP

Hard stop 4. Thresholds table under LOOM ORCHESTRATION REFERENCE.

### 3b. POST-COMPACTION RECOVERY

After any compaction or context loss: 1) `loom memory list`; 2) check `.work/handoffs/` for your stage's latest handoff; 3) read it and resume where the previous context left off. Do not start fresh.

### 4. COMMIT AND COMPLETE (HOOK-ENFORCED)

Before ending any loom worktree session (hard stop 3):

```bash
git add <specific-files> && git commit -m "feat(scope): <description>"
loom stage complete <stage-id>   # from the worktree root
```

**Complete ONLY a settled stage — completion is the session's LAST act.** Before `loom stage complete`: every subagent, coordinator, team, and Workflow you spawned has returned and its result is absorbed (or you deliberately abandoned it and recorded why) — nothing is still running or expected to report; every defect found by review or verification is fixed and re-verified — nothing is left open or merely "reported"; all work is committed and `git status` is clean. Waiting on a live subagent is never a reason to complete — wait it out (Rule 6). After `loom stage complete` succeeds, STOP and end the session: no further edits, spawns, checks, or fixes. The merge starts from the completed commit, so post-completion work is LOST WORK. If you catch yourself wanting to do anything after completing, you completed too early — the fix is to complete later, never to keep working past it.

Commits follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/): `type(scope): description` with `feat` | `fix` | `refactor` | `test` | `docs` | `chore`. Group logically — separate commits for a new module, its tests, wiring, docs. 5 files across 3 concerns = 3 commits, not 1 or 5.

### 5. SUBAGENT RESTRICTIONS

Subagents never run `git commit`, `loom stage complete`, or `git add -A`/`git add .` (hard stop 3) — subagent commits cause lost work. Subagents write code and report; the main agent owns verification and all git operations.

Include as the first lines of EVERY subagent prompt:

```text
** READ CLAUDE.md IMMEDIATELY AND FOLLOW ALL ITS RULES. **

SUBAGENT RESTRICTIONS - YOU ARE A SUBAGENT:
- NEVER run git commit - the main agent will commit your work
- NEVER run loom stage complete - the main agent will complete the stage
- NEVER run git add -A or git add . - the main agent handles staging
- DO write code and report results back

VERIFICATION IS THE MAIN AGENT'S JOB - NOT YOURS:
- Do NOT verify your work. No full build, no full test suite, no linter, no
  formatter, no type-checker, and never a repeated or looping check.
- AT MOST ONE narrowly-scoped check over the files YOU wrote (e.g.
  `cargo test <your_module>::`), run ONCE. Skip it if you are unsure.
- Report instead: files changed, assumptions made, anything unresolved.
  The MAIN AGENT compiles, tests, lints, and fixes.
- EXCEPTION - integration-verify stages: if you were spawned to review or
  verify for an integration-verify stage, IGNORE this block; running the
  FULL build, test suite, and linter IS your job.

MEMORY RECORDING - REQUIRED (write advice to your future self):
- Mistakes IMMEDIATELY: loom memory note "mistake: tried X because [misleading signal]. Failed because Y. Prevention: [detection rule]. Fix: Z"
- Non-obvious decisions: loom memory decision "chose X over Y" --context "because Z"
- Surprises/gotchas: loom memory note "found/gotcha: ... in file:line"
- Stale knowledge IMMEDIATELY: loom memory note "stale-knowledge: <knowledge file>#<heading> claims X; the tree does Y (file:line). Correction: <replacement text>"
- Do NOT record procedural actions ("spawned agents", "read files", "ran tests")

AUTO-MEMORY PROHIBITION:
- ONLY loom memory commands - NEVER Claude Code auto-memory
- NEVER Write/Edit files under ~/.claude/projects/*/memory/ (invisible to loom)
```

### 6. EXECUTING PARALLEL SUBAGENTS

For non-trivial work, spawn parallel subagents for independent file changes, separate implementation areas, tests alongside implementation, and exploration.

If your stage has an `EXECUTION PLAN` block, parse ALL subagent assignments and spawn ALL in ONE message (multiple Task calls) — parallel means parallel. Each prompt includes: the Rule 5 preamble, assignment, files owned, files read-only, acceptance criteria, task detail. "Sequential" in the plan means execute in order with explicit dependencies.

File exclusivity: each subagent gets exclusive write access to its files — two subagents writing one file = lost work. If overlap is unavoidable, serialize in one subagent or split stages. Include a file-ownership table in every prompt:

| Subagent            | Files Owned (write) | Files Read-Only |
| ------------------- | ------------------- | --------------- |
| Subagent 1 — [role] | `path/to/files`     | `shared/config` |
| Subagent 2 — [role] | `other/files`       | `shared/config` |

Hierarchical EXECUTION PLANs: spawn the COORDINATORS (not workers) in one message; each spawns its own workers (Rule 6c).

**Check on subagents at a bounded cadence — a check firing is NOT a deadline on the subagent's work.** Never block open-endedly on a single wait: any watcher, poll loop, or background wait you arm MUST have its own deadline of 300s or less, MUST terminate on BOTH branches — result AND no-result-yet — reporting which fired, and MUST carry a liveness signal (task still running? files changing?) so "slow" and "dead" are distinguishable. `subagent_timeout_secs` (300s built-in default) is the stage's advisory heartbeat budget — a monitoring cadence, not a time limit on subagents.

When a check fires and the subagent is still alive — the harness reports its task running, or anything progressed since the last check — the ONLY correct move is to re-arm the check and keep waiting. That is the normal case, not a failure: slow is not dead, and a subagent gets as long as its task takes. Take over or re-assign ONLY on positive evidence of death — the task failed or was killed, or several consecutive checks show zero liveness AND no result. Elapsed time alone is NEVER that evidence. Restarting live work forfeits every token it has spent and sets two agents writing the same files; both cost more than any wait. And never answer a slow subagent by completing the stage: a stage with any subagent still out is not settled (Rule 4).

### 6b. AGENT TEAMS (WHEN AVAILABLE)

Teams add inter-agent messaging, shared task lists, and dynamic work claiming, at roughly 7x whole-job cost. Use ONLY when work is wide and exploratory and genuinely needs inter-agent communication or dynamic task discovery — never for concrete assignments with clear file ownership (use Rule 6 or 6c).

Team lead: only you run `git commit`, `loom stage complete`, and `loom memory`. Delegate implementation; keep context for coordination (<40%); shut down ALL teammates before completing the stage. Flow: TeamCreate → TaskCreate work items → spawn teammates → monitor (TaskList) → shutdown, verify, complete.

### 6c. SUBAGENT HIERARCHIES (2-LEVEL CAP)

Loom caps agent trees at TWO levels: main agent → coordinators → workers. Workers NEVER spawn subagents.

Use a hierarchy when ALL hold: the work is well-defined enough for sonnet workers; it splits into 2-4 disjoint file territories; each territory has 2+ worker tasks; flat fan-out would mean the main agent authoring more than ~6 prompts, absorbing N raw results, or serializing waves. Do not use one when: total worker tasks ≤ ~6 (flat is simpler and cheaper), territories share files (separate stages), cross-territory iteration is needed (teams), or the work is sequential.

Model mix: implementation is delegated to as FEW subagents as the work allows, each at the CHEAPEST tier that can do its piece, picked PER SUBAGENT by what that piece of work needs, never once for the whole stage: codex gpt-5.6-luna for boilerplate, scaffolding, and simple unit tests; SONNET (`loom-software-engineer`) or codex gpt-5.6-terra for common implementation and integration tests (the default lane — most workers belong here); OPUS for mainstream architecture and algorithm implementation (`loom-senior-software-engineer`); FABLE only for visual/UI design, a bug that survived a delegated fix, and extremely challenging algorithmic design (no agent type pins fable — pass the model override explicitly at spawn). Coordinators delegate for the same reason the main agent does (hard stop 6): a coordinator running an expensive model still writes briefs, not implementations. Codex tiers (effort xhigh, via `loom-codex-forwarder`) exist only on stages listing codex in `implementers` — otherwise that work goes to sonnet. Spawn workers BY AGENT TYPE; an untyped worker inherits the main session model, silently making every worker opus on an opus stage. Lanes mix within one stage and even within one wave: pick each worker's lane by what its task needs, and keep ONE file-ownership table covering every worker in every lane. Opus coordinator only when integrating its territory needs architectural judgment.

COORDINATOR PREAMBLE — first lines of every coordinator prompt (standalone; do not prepend Rule 5's):

```text
** READ CLAUDE.md IMMEDIATELY AND FOLLOW ALL ITS RULES. **

COORDINATOR ROLE - YOU ARE A SUBAGENT COORDINATING WORKERS (ONE LEVEL ONLY):
- You own ONE territory: [TERRITORY]. Never touch files outside it.
- Partition your territory into DISJOINT worker file sets - two workers writing one file = LOST WORK
- Spawn workers via the Task tool BY AGENT TYPE (loom-software-engineer = sonnet); include the WORKER PREAMBLE as the first lines of EVERY worker prompt; spawn independent workers in ONE message
- Workers NEVER spawn subagents - they are LEAVES (loom caps the tree at 2 levels)
- Delegate implementation; write at most small glue/fixes within your territory
- AT MOST ONE narrowly-scoped check over the files your workers wrote (e.g. `cargo test <your_module>::`), run ONCE; skip it if you are unsure. The MAIN AGENT compiles, tests, lints, and fixes.
- Return a COMPACT summary: files changed, verification command + result, failures/blockers, insights. No file dumps, no diffs.
- NEVER run git commit, git add -A/., or loom stage complete - only the main agent does
- Record insights via loom memory note/decision; NEVER use Claude Code auto-memory
```

WORKER PREAMBLE — Rule 5 preamble first, then append:

```text
WORKER RESTRICTIONS - YOU ARE A LEAF AGENT:
- NEVER spawn subagents (Task tool) - the hierarchy is capped at 2 levels; do the work yourself
- Touch ONLY your assigned files: [FILES]
- Return a short report: what changed, anything surprising, anything unresolved

VERIFICATION IS THE MAIN AGENT'S JOB - NOT YOURS:
- Do NOT verify your work. No full build, no full test suite, no linter, no
  formatter, no type-checker, and never a repeated or looping check.
- AT MOST ONE narrowly-scoped check over the files YOU wrote (e.g.
  `cargo test <your_module>::`), run ONCE. Skip it if you are unsure.
- Report instead: files changed, assumptions made, anything unresolved.
  The MAIN AGENT compiles, tests, lints, and fixes.
```

### 7. SKILLS, AGENTS, AND MODEL CHOICE

Check available skills and agents before implementing; the prompt-submit hook surfaces matching `/loom-*` skills — invoke the best match before writing code.

| Agent                           | Model  | Use for                                                                                                                                                           |
| ------------------------------- | ------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `loom-software-engineer`        | Sonnet | Common implementation and integration tests to detailed instructions; refactors to spec                                                                           |
| `loom-senior-software-engineer` | Opus   | Mainstream architecture, algorithm implementation, complex debugging, security-sensitive code, cross-cutting refactors                                            |
| `loom-code-reviewer`            | —      | Read-only code/security/architecture review                                                                                                                       |
| `Explore`                       | —      | Codebase exploration                                                                                                                                              |
| `loom-advisor`                  | Fable  | Read-only advisory for debugging and repeated failures — receives full detail from the orchestrator, returns a diagnosis and a concrete next step, writes nothing |
| `loom-codex-forwarder`           | gpt-5.6-terra / gpt-5.6-luna | Terra for common implementation and integration tests, luna for boilerplate, scaffolding, and simple unit tests, on stages listing codex in `implementers` - loom forwarding shim over the codex plugin runtime; state `--model <tier model> --effort xhigh` in the prompt; write-capable by default; foreground only; never verifies. May run alongside Claude subagents in the same stage |

If the codex CLI or plugin is missing, loom warns at `loom run` startup and the codex tiers' work falls back to sonnet.

Fable-tier work (visual/UI design, a bug that survived a delegated fix, extremely challenging algorithmic or architectural design) has no pinned agent type — pass the model override explicitly at spawn. Fable is the most expensive tier: reach for it when the *thinking* is the hard part, and let it produce a design, diagnosis, or brief that a cheaper tier executes.

Model allocation:

1. THE MAIN AGENT NEVER IMPLEMENTS — WHATEVER MODEL IT RUNS (hard stop 6).
   Every stage's main agent is an orchestrator: it decomposes the work, hands
   each subagent full context, then verifies and commits. That is all. This
   holds identically for an opus session and a fable session; a session running
   an expensive model is MORE obliged to delegate, not less.
2. INVESTIGATION ENDS IN A BRIEF, NOT IN AN EDIT. The moment you finish reading
   the code and know what the fix is, you are at the delegation boundary — that
   understanding is exactly what makes a cheap subagent effective. Write it down
   (file:line, root cause, the change to make, signatures, patterns to match,
   acceptance) and spawn. Do not slide from "I have diagnosed it" into "I will
   just type it"; the diagnosis being yours does not make the typing yours.
3. IMPLEMENTATION IS ALWAYS DELEGATED, to as FEW subagents as the work allows, at
   the CHEAPEST tier that can do the piece. Pick PER SUBAGENT by what that piece
   needs, never once for the whole stage, and default downward: codex
   gpt-5.6-luna for boilerplate, scaffolding, and simple unit tests; SONNET
   (loom-software-engineer) or codex gpt-5.6-terra for common implementation and
   integration tests — this is the default lane and most work belongs here; OPUS
   (loom-senior-software-engineer) for mainstream architecture and algorithm
   implementation; FABLE only for visual/UI design, a bug that survived a
   delegated fix attempt, or extremely challenging algorithmic design. Codex
   tiers (effort xhigh, via loom-codex-forwarder) exist only on stages listing
   codex in implementers AND when the codex CLI + plugin are installed;
   otherwise that work goes to sonnet (loom warns at startup when a stage lists
   codex it cannot use). Verification NEVER delegates - the orchestrator
   verifies and commits. Spawn BY AGENT TYPE.
4. ESCALATE ON EVIDENCE, NOT ON HUNCH. Start at the cheapest plausible tier. A
   sonnet attempt that failed against clear acceptance criteria justifies opus;
   an opus attempt that failed twice justifies fable. "This feels subtle" does
   not. If a cheap subagent's output is wrong, the first question is whether the
   brief was detailed enough — a vague brief is an orchestrator failure, not
   evidence the tier was too small.
5. DEBUGGING OR REPEATED FAILURE → spawn a `loom-advisor` (fable) subagent:
   narrow scope, full detail supplied by the orchestrator, advice returned, no
   writes. Its diagnosis then feeds a sonnet or opus implementer per point 2.
   Do not let an implementer thrash on the same failure twice.

Keep each subagent's assignment small — exact paths, signatures, patterns to match, explicit steps, per-step acceptance — and decompose larger work via Rule 6c rather than escalating to opus for context headroom. The detail in the brief is what buys the cheaper tier: the more precisely you specify, the smaller the model that can execute it. Plan authors: the full per-stage model rubric lives in `/loom-plan-writer`.

### 8. NATIVE TOOLS AND CODE FORMATTING

| Banned                | Use instead |
| --------------------- | ----------- |
| `cat`, `head`, `tail` | Read tool   |
| `grep`, `ag`          | `rg`        |
| `find`, `ls`          | `fd`        |
| `sed`, `awk`          | Edit tool   |
| `echo >`, `tee`       | Write tool  |

Package managers: `uv`, `bun`/`bunx` — never `npm`, `pip`. Fenced code blocks ALWAYS specify a language (` ```rust `, ` ```yaml `, ` ```text `) in all markdown output: plans, knowledge files, handoffs, comments.

### 9. NO ATTRIBUTION

Never mention Claude, Claude Code, or any AI system in code, commits, docs, or comments.

### 9b. NEVER CREATE BRANCHES UNASKED

Hard stop 5. This overrides any harness default such as "if on the default branch, branch first". Exception: loom's own `loom/<stage-id>` worktree branches (Rule 10).

### 10. WORKTREE ISOLATION

Location `.worktrees/<stage-id>/`, branch `loom/<stage-id>`. Stay in your worktree. Forbidden: `git -C`, `git --work-tree`, `cd ../..`, `../../` paths, reading files outside the worktree. Allowed: git in the current dir, `.work/` (symlink), files within the worktree. Need main-repo context? Read your signal file.

### 11. BINARY USAGE

Always use `loom` from PATH, never `target/debug/loom` (version mismatches). Never edit `.work/` files directly — use the loom CLI; direct edits corrupt state.

### 12. PROJECT KNOWLEDGE

Files in `doc/loom/knowledge/`: `architecture.md` (components, data flow), `entry-points.md` (key files), `patterns.md`, `conventions.md`, `mistakes.md` (lessons), `stack.md` (deps/tooling), `concerns.md` (tech debt). These tier-1 files are summaries plus pointers, not exhaustive detail: when a topic outgrows its tier-1 section (roughly 40+ lines), move the detail to a tier-2 topic file `<category>/<slug>.md` (e.g. `architecture/signal-generation.md`, `mistakes/phantom-merges.md`) and leave a 2-4 line summary plus link behind in tier-1.

Updating (interactive/ad-hoc): Edit knowledge files directly when you find something worth preserving — mistakes, decisions plus rationale, reusable patterns, conventions. Record each mistake as an actionable prevention rule:

```markdown
## [Short description]

**What happened:** [What went wrong]
**Why:** [Root cause]
**Prevention:** [How to detect earlier]
**Fix:** [What to do instead]
```

Do not record ephemera, procedural steps, or anything derivable from git history or the code. During loom execution, use the `loom memory`/`loom knowledge` CLI as your signal instructs — do not edit knowledge files directly: `loom knowledge update <category>/<slug>` writes a tier-2 topic file. `INDEX.md` is regenerated automatically on every knowledge write, so there is no final index step to run.

**Stale knowledge is a defect — fix it where you find it.** A knowledge file that contradicts the tree is worse than no file: it is quoted verbatim into every later stage's Knowledge Brief, so one wrong line misleads every agent the plan spawns. The moment you catch one — a command that does not exist, a claim the code disproves, a "(New, Stage N+)" note for something that shipped, a section whose successor was appended below it — stop and route it:

| Where you are                     | What to do with the stale claim                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Executing a loom stage (any type) | `loom memory note "stale-knowledge: <file>#<heading> claims X; the tree does Y (file:line). Correction: <the replacement text>"` — then carry on with your own task. Do NOT edit knowledge files (the worktree guard blocks file tools on them) and do NOT run `loom knowledge update` unless you are a knowledge stage. |
| knowledge-distill stage           | Apply EVERY `stale-knowledge:` memory with `loom knowledge replace-section`, BEFORE curating anything new (see below).                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
| Interactive / outside a plan      | Correct it NOW, in place — Edit the section directly, or `loom knowledge replace-section`. Creating a NEW tier-2 topic file goes through `loom knowledge update <category>/<slug>` (it scaffolds the file; the Write tool is denied in some projects). Never leave stale text standing above its own correction.         |

**Correct in place with `replace-section`, never with `update`.** `loom knowledge update` APPENDS: using it to fix a section leaves the wrong text directly above the right text, and the next reader scanning top-down hits the wrong one first.

```bash
loom knowledge replace-section patterns "Merge Flow" "<corrected body, WITHOUT the ## heading line>"
loom knowledge replace-section mistakes/phantom-merges "What Happened" -   # body from stdin
```

It overwrites the body under the first `## <heading>` up to the next `## ` heading, and when the heading is absent it appends the section and SAYS so — read that line: an appended "correction" means your heading did not match and the stale text is still there. Name the wrong claim explicitly in the replacement ("there is no `loom knowledge audit`; duplicate headings are found with ..."), because a silent deletion invites the next agent to re-add it from the same stale source.

**knowledge-distill: corrections come first.** Sweep the memories for `stale-knowledge:` and apply every one before curating anything new. An unapplied `stale-knowledge:` memory is a correction LOST when the plan completes — memory is discarded, the knowledge base stays wrong, and the next plan inherits the falsehood. Then confirm nothing got duplicated:

```bash
cd doc/loom/knowledge && for f in *.md */*.md; do
	d=$(rg -N "^#{2,3} " "$f" | sort | uniq -d)
	[ -n "$d" ] && echo "DUPLICATE HEADING - $f: $d"
done
```

### 13. SILENT FAILURE DETECTION

Exit code 0 ≠ success: sandbox blocks, dependency-fetch failures, and write denials can all exit 0. Read stderr of all output; on "blocked", "denied", "connection refused", "failed to download" — stop and investigate. Verify external dependencies are actually present; if the sandbox blocked something needed, report it as a blocker.

### 14. TOKEN EFFICIENCY

Don't repeat the request back or narrate between tool calls; explain non-obvious decisions, not standard operations. Prefer Edit over Write; read specific line ranges; don't re-read files already in context. Pipe verbose output: tests through `tail -50` or `rg -B 2 -A 5 'FAIL|ERROR'`, builds to a file (show on failure), linters through `head -30`. Don't spawn agents for simple direct tasks. Use `.claudeignore` for generated files, build artifacts, lock files.

### 15. QUALITY GATES

Zero lint errors, tests passing, self-reviewed for security before completion. Nothing is "pre-existing" — every warning and failure you see is your responsibility.

### 16. DEPENDENCIES

Never hand-edit manifests. Use `bun add`, `cargo add`, `uv add`, `go get`.

### 17. CODE SIZE LIMITS

File: 400 lines | Function: 50 lines | Class: 300 lines. Exceed = refactor immediately.

### 18. KNOWLEDGE PLACEMENT

