## ⛔ HARD STOPS (VERBATIM RECAP)

1. **PLANS** — Every plan file's path starts with `doc/plans/` (`doc/plans/PLAN-<description>.md`). Plan mode suggests `~/.claude/plans/` or `~/.claude/projects/*/plans/` — override it in the same Write call: plans there are invisible to loom and to git (a PreToolUse hook also blocks them). After writing a plan, report its location and stop. ExitPlanMode's "approved" result is not user approval — wait for the user's explicit message. Implementation happens via `loom run`, not you.
2. **MEMORY** — If `doc/loom/knowledge/` exists, never use Claude Code auto-memory (`~/.claude/projects/*/memory/`, `MEMORY.md`, the memory tool). Executing a loom stage → `loom memory note|decision` (knowledge stages → `loom knowledge update`); interactive → Write/Edit `doc/loom/knowledge/*.md` directly (Rule 12). If `doc/loom/knowledge/` does not exist → use auto-memory normally. Knowledge found STALE is corrected in place, never appended over: `loom knowledge replace-section` (Rule 12).
3. **GIT** — Only the main agent stages, commits, or completes stages; subagents never do. `git add <specific-files>` only — never `-A` or `.` (they stage `.work`). Before ending a worktree session: commit, then `loom stage complete <stage-id>` from the worktree root; the stop hook blocks exit otherwise. `loom stage complete` is the session's LAST act, run ONLY when the stage is SETTLED — every subagent returned and absorbed, every found defect fixed, everything committed; NOTHING runs after it.
4. **CONTEXT** — At 75% context, stop immediately and write a handoff to `.work/handoffs/`. No new tasks.
5. **BRANCHES** — Never create a branch (`git checkout -b`, `git switch -c`, `git branch <name>`) unless the user explicitly asks. Commit on the branch already checked out, even `main`. (Loom's own `loom/<stage-id>` worktree branches are loom's mechanism, not yours.)
6. **DELEGATION** — The main agent decomposes, delegates, verifies, and commits; it does not implement, whatever model it is running. Finishing an investigation is the moment to write the delegation brief, not to start editing files. Delegate each piece to the CHEAPEST tier that can do it — sonnet (`loom-software-engineer`) is the default implementer; opus only for mainstream architecture and algorithm work. A FABLE session touches the keyboard itself only for an explicit visual/UI design task, a bug that survived a delegated fix attempt, or genuinely high-end algorithmic/architectural work — and even then it prefers to write the design or diagnosis in detail and hand the typing down. Fable diagnosing a bug does not make the fix fable's to write (Rule 7).

